            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Boston to Ireland?</h3>
            <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, typically $400 to $800 round-trip. For summer visits, book 45 to 70 days ahead and target April or September. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend pricing throughout the year.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Boston to Ireland?</h3>
            <p>Aer Lingus and Delta both operate daily year-round nonstop flights from Boston to Dublin. Aer Lingus also offers seasonal service to Shannon. Flight duration is approximately six hours. Pre-clearance at Dublin saves time upon return to Boston.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit Ireland from the US?</h3>
            <p>United States citizens do not require a visa for short tourist stays in Ireland. You may stay up to 90 days. A valid passport is required. Ireland is outside the Schengen Area, so separate entry requirements apply to travel to Continental Europe.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Boston to Ireland flight?</h3>
            <p>Most full-service carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds, one carry-on, and a personal item on transatlantic itineraries. Aer Lingus includes baggage on Smart fares. Always verify your specific airline's policy before packing for Ireland.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Dublin Airport to central Dublin?</h3>
            <p>The Aircoach express bus costs €10 and reaches the city center in 25 to 40 minutes. Dublin Bus routes 16 and 41 cost €3.30 but take 40 to 60 minutes. Taxis and Uber run €25 to €35 and take 20 to 30 minutes depending on traffic.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Ireland, or do I need euros?</h3>
            <p>Ireland uses the euro exclusively, and US dollars are not accepted. ATMs are abundant throughout Dublin, and credit cards work at most establishments. Contactless payment is widely preferred. Carry some cash for smaller purchases at pubs and local shops.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Ireland safe for American tourists?</h3>
            <p>Ireland is generally very safe for visitors, though petty theft occurs near Temple Bar, Grafton Street, and on crowded public transport. Watch for pickpockets and phone snatching. Violent crime against tourists is rare, and police presence remains visible in tourist areas.</p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Dublin for first-time visitors?</h3>
            <p>Temple Bar places you near pubs and nightlife but can be noisy. St. Stephen's Green offers elegant surroundings and park access. The Docklands suits business travelers and modern architecture fans. Rathmines provides a local atmosphere with excellent dining and vintage charm.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Boston Logan for my Ireland flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from BOS Terminal C or E. During peak summer travel or holiday weekends, consider arriving three and a half hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can reduce security wait times.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Ireland is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must provide meals and accommodation for qualifying overnight delays under EU regulations for flights departing Dublin, and most extend comparable protections for flights to Ireland. Contact your airline immediately and maintain comprehensive travel insurance coverage for maximum protection.</p>













